ow  some love this scene above all other  versions and some don't but I want to  make one thing clear it's not wrong to  prefer any version over another we enjoy  what we enjoy with that said the  argument surrounds Goku's dialogue and  the way his character is portrayed for  example in the Japanese he says Mae amor  I am at the unison ISM the scene depicts  Goku as unforgiving he has snapped the  carefree Goku has given way to this more  aggressive and angry incarnation of the  character he even snaps at Gohan earlier  in the episode in the English version  they decide to reimagine the character  this is that same scene but in English  your evil deeds are like a noose around  your neck the script is going for a more  righteous interpretation of the Goku  character and nowhere is this more clear  than in the now-famous I am speech at  the end of this episode it reads I am  the hope of the universe I am the answer  to all living things that cry out for  peace  I am protector of the innocent I am the  light in the darkness I am truth  echoing in the background is the Bruce  Falconer score giving it a distinctive  atmosphere from its original version  which utilized the Kikuchi score both of  these respective soundtracks have  enormous fan bases that will vehemently  argue that their version is better than  all others and so with that one done and  out of the way let's jump forward to  Dragon Ball Kai original release  February 21st and November 17th 2010  entitled the angry Super Saiyan Goku  throws down the gauntlet this episode  was redubbed and released on the 21st of  February 2010 in Japan and later that  same year on November 17th in English  the driving forces behind the Dragon  Ball Kai Reed above the series was to  essentially have a recut streamlined  version of the original run of the  Dragon Ball anime aiming to could have  much of the filler portions to the story  were possible and in this particular  episode the filler scenes with Roshi and  the gang wer